{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1300854","title":"Floc Size Driven Fungal Community Dynamics in a Full Scale Landfill Leachate Treatment System","description":"This project explores how floc size influences fungal community dynamics during the upgrading of a nitrogen removal process in a full-scale landfill leachate treatment plant. Samples were collected across three operational stages: conventional nitrification denitrification (CND), transitional (Trans), and partial nitrification denitrification (PND). Fungal communities from large (L), medium (M), and small (S) flocs were analyzed using high throughput internal transcribed spacer (ITS) sequencing. The study investigates changes in community composition, assembly mechanisms, source contributions, and network structures. Results show that small flocs consistently contributed most to the supernatant community and were influenced more by stochastic processes, while larger flocs showed stronger ecological selection. Co occurrence networks shifted from small world to scale free modular structures after process upgrading. This is the first study to link floc size stratification with fungal ecology in engineered wastewater systems, providing new insights into spatial microbial organization and system optimization strategies.","organism":null,"data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"Guangdong Pharmaceutical University","submission_date":"2025/08/03 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMN50429349","MIMARKS Survey related sample from activated sludge metagenome","2016-09","China","Guangdong","Guangzhou",23.1291,113.2644,"non_urban",null,"BioProject indicat